WEBVTT

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:04.265
Aerial observation is structured, purpose-driven visual collection from above.

00:00:04.265 --> 00:00:08.150
It can use drones, aircraft, towers, elevated cameras, or overhead imagery,

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:11.780
but every method requires context, authority, and careful interpretation.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:15.090
Begin with a question, bounded area, required detail, time window,

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.950
environmental constraints, and explicit exclusions.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:22.005
Plan rules, airspace, property access, weather, lighting, obstacles, route,

00:00:22.005 --> 00:00:24.765
battery margins, recovery, and emergency options.

00:00:24.765 --> 00:00:28.450
Collect stable, well-framed imagery with an angle, field of view, overlap,

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:30.220
and format suited to the objective.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:34.225
Describe visible features before interpreting them, and never infer identity, intent,

00:00:34.225 --> 00:00:37.360
ownership, cause, or severity without supporting evidence.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.330
Time-based comparison depends on repeatable collection and preserved date, location,

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:43.030
sensor, and condition context.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:47.410
File naming, metadata, logs, source notes, controlled originals, access restrictions,

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.290
and retention make findings reviewable.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:51.645
The goal is useful situational awareness and evidence,

00:00:51.645 --> 00:00:54.300
not uncontrolled surveillance or instant certainty.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.160
Strong practice combines safety, image quality, documentation, privacy, proportionality,

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.880
and responsible reporting.
